      {
        "id": "example",
        "type": "worked_example",
        "provenance": "original",
        "problem": {
          "en": "If alpha, beta are the roots of x^2 + 7x + 10 = 0, find alpha^2 + beta^2 and alpha/beta + beta/alpha."
        },
        "steps": [
          {
            "text": {
              "en": "Here a = 1, b = 7, c = 10, so alpha + beta = -7 and alpha x beta = 10."
            },
            "why": {
              "en": "Sum = -b/a, product = c/a."
            }
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "en": "alpha^2 + beta^2 = (alpha + beta)^2 - 2 alpha beta = 49 - 20 = 29."
            },
            "why": {
              "en": "Use the identity."
            }
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "en": "alpha/beta + beta/alpha = (alpha^2 + beta^2) / (alpha beta) = 29/10."
            },
            "why": {
              "en": "Common denominator."
            }
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "en": "So the answers are 29 and 29/10."
            },
            "why": {
              "en": "Check with the roots -2 and -5: 4 + 25 = 29."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "teachback",
        "type": "teach_back",
        "provenance": "original",
        "prompt": {
          "en": "The difference between the roots of x^2 - 13x + k = 0 is 17. Find k."
        },
        "requiredIdeas": [
          {
            "en": "alpha + beta = 13 and alpha - beta = 17"
          },
          {
            "en": "Adding: 2 alpha = 30, so alpha = 15 and beta = -2"
          },
          {
            "en": "k = alpha x beta = 15 x (-2) = -30"
          }
